{#
  defra-internal — header

  The Defra top bar: the organisation lock-up, the signed-in person and the way
  out.

  It pairs with govuk-frontend's own service navigation, rendered next to this
  one rather than inside it — the same way the GOV.UK header and service
  navigation are two components combined on the page. The service name, the
  links and the regulator the person works for all belong to that component,
  not to this one.

  The account menu is a list, not a bespoke user object, so it takes the shape a
  service already builds for the service navigation: { text, href, current }. An
  item with an `href` is a link; one without is plain text, which is how the
  signed-in person's name renders. Pass nothing and no menu renders, which is
  what a signed-out page needs. There is no sign-in param: this header belongs
  to pages you are already on, so signing in belongs on a page, not the chrome.

  The param is `navigation` because that is what this shape is called
  everywhere. A service that already has a `navigation` feeding
  govukServiceNavigation builds a SECOND list for this one — call it
  `accountNavigation` — and passes `navigation: accountNavigation`. Two lists,
  because they are two things: places to go, and who you are. Worked example in
  app/lib/navigation.js.

  There is no `html` key beside `text`. Nothing in an account menu is markup.

  params:
    organisationName  {string}  logo lock-up text; use <br> to control wrapping
    homepageUrl       {string}  where the lock-up links to (default "/")
    navigation        {array}   [{ text, href, current, visuallyHiddenText }]
                                shown top right; omit on a signed-out page to
                                hide the account menu. No `href` renders plain
                                text. `current` sets aria-current="page", the
                                same as govuk-frontend. `visuallyHiddenText` is
                                an optional screen-reader prefix, e.g.
                                "Signed in as " before a person's name.

  Styles: app/assets/sass/_defra-internal.scss
#}
{# No `role="banner"`. A top-level `<header>` already is one, and govuk-frontend
   dropped the explicit role from its own header for the same reason. #}
<header class="defra-internal-header">
  <div class="govuk-width-container">
    <div class="defra-internal-header__main">
      <div class="defra-internal-header__logo">
        <a class="defra-internal-header__logo-link" href="{{ params.homepageUrl | default('/') }}">
          {{ params.organisationName | default("Department for Environment,<br>Food &amp; Rural Affairs") | safe }}
        </a>
      </div>
      {# A div, not a nav. The GOV.UK header marks its navigation up as `nav`
         because it is a set of destinations; a name and one action is not. #}
      {% if params.navigation.length %}
        <div class="defra-internal-header__account-menu">
          <ul>
            {% for item in params.navigation %}
              {# Captured once and used in both branches, the way govuk-frontend's
                 own service navigation builds its items. `safe` on output only
                 prevents double-escaping — both values are escaped at capture. #}
              {%- set itemContent %}{% if item.visuallyHiddenText %}<span class="govuk-visually-hidden">{{ item.visuallyHiddenText }}</span>{% endif %}{{ item.text }}{% endset -%}
              <li>{% if item.href %}<a href="{{ item.href }}"{% if item.current %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ itemContent | safe }}</a>{% else %}{{ itemContent | safe }}{% endif %}</li>
            {% endfor %}
          </ul>
        </div>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  </div>
</header>
